/-!
# The slash of a cusp form on $\Gamma_1(N)$ by an element of $\Gamma_0(N)$ is again a cusp form on $\Gamma_1(N)$

Theorem statement: `MTT.exists_cuspForm_slash_gamma0` (`73a0c211-8cd0-444b-ac39-d39d363a8e88`), by
cbirkbeck, 2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `31248ea3-784e-4ee3-8361-5f8e445f3239`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-06 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $N\ge1$, $k\ge0$, $g\in S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$ and $\gamma=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\
c&d\end{pmatrix}\in\Gamma_0(N)$. Then the weight-$k$ slash $g|_k\gamma$, i.e. the function $z\mapsto
(cz+d)^{-k}g(\gamma z)$, is again a cusp form of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_1(N)$. Formally: there is
$g'\in S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$ with
$$g(\gamma z)=(cz+d)^k\,g'(z)\qquad\text{for all }z\in\mathfrak H .$$

*Why this holds.* $\Gamma_1(N)$ is the kernel of the homomorphism $\Gamma_0(N)\to(\mathbf
Z/N)^\times$, $\gamma\mapsto d\bmod N$, hence is normal in $\Gamma_0(N)$; so for
$\delta\in\Gamma_1(N)$ one has
$(g|_k\gamma)|_k\delta=g|_k(\gamma\delta\gamma^{-1})|_k\gamma=g|_k\gamma$. Holomorphy is preserved
by the slash action, and $g|_k\gamma$ vanishes at every cusp $\mathfrak c$ of $\Gamma_1(N)$ because
$\gamma\mathfrak c$ is again a cusp and $g$ vanishes there. (This is the diamond operator $\langle
d\rangle$ on $S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$; Diamond–Shurman §5.2.)

## Explanation of the source proof

We show that for $g\in S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$ and $\gamma=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\
c&d\end{pmatrix}\in\Gamma_0(N)$ the slash $g|_k\gamma$ is a cusp form on $\Gamma_1(N)$, i.e. there
is $g'\in S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$ with $g(\gamma z)=(cz+d)^kg'(z)$. This is the diamond operator $\langle
d\rangle$ of Diamond–Shurman §5.2.

The cusp form is constructed directly (`slashCuspForm`) with underlying function $g|_k\gamma$
(Mathlib's weight-$k$ slash by the image of $\gamma$ in $GL_2(\mathbf R)$).

*Invariance.* $\Gamma_1(N)$ is the image of the kernel of the homomorphism $\Gamma_0(N)\to\mathbf
Z/N$, $\gamma\mapsto d\bmod N$ (Mathlib's `Gamma1' N = (Gamma0Map N).ker`), so it is normalised by
$\Gamma_0(N)$: for $\delta\in\Gamma_1(N)$ we have $\gamma\delta\gamma^{-1}\in\Gamma_1(N)$
(`conj_mem_Gamma1`, from `MonoidHom.normal_ker`). Hence
$(g|_k\gamma)|_k\delta=g|_k(\gamma\delta)=g|_k\big((\gamma\delta\gamma^{-1})\gamma\big)=(g|_k\gamma\delta\gamma^{-1})|_k\gamma=g|_k\gamma$,
using that the slash is an action (`SlashAction.slash_mul`) and the invariance of $g$ under
$\Gamma_1(N)$.

*Holomorphy* is preserved by the slash action (`MDifferentiable.slash`).

*Cusps.* If $\mathfrak c$ is a cusp of $\Gamma_1(N)$ and $h\in GL_2(\mathbf R)$ sends $\infty$ to
$\mathfrak c$, then $(g|_k\gamma)|_kh=g|_k(\gamma h)$ and $\gamma h$ sends $\infty$ to
$\gamma\mathfrak c$, which is again a cusp of $\Gamma_1(N)$ because $\Gamma_1(N)$ is arithmetic and
conjugation by a rational matrix preserves arithmetic subgroups (`isCusp_toGL_smul`, via
`IsCusp.smul`, `Subgroup.IsArithmetic.conj` and `isCusp_iff_isCusp_SL2Z`); so the slash vanishes at
$i\infty$ since $g$ vanishes at $\gamma\mathfrak c$.

Finally, unwinding the slash formula $(g|_k\gamma)(z)=g(\gamma z)(cz+d)^{-k}$ (`slash_mapGL_apply`,
with $\det\gamma=1$ and $cz+d\ne0$) gives the displayed identity.

